  Four further mentions are PROSE, inside comments, and are not declarations:
  ci.yml:8, ci.yml:176, binary-release.yml:56, binary-release.yml:100,
  release.yml:208.

### 3. what the fleet can serve

  $ gh api orgs/paiml/actions/runners --jq '[.runners[] | .labels[].name] | group_by(.) | map({label:.[0],count:length}) | sort_by(-.count)[]'
  Linux 25 · self-hosted 25 · build 23 · X64 21 · clean-room 20 · intel 16
  docker 7 · ephemeral 7 · yoga 5 · ARM64 4 · cuda 4 · gpu 4 · gx10 4
  ada 1 · blackwell 1 · gb10 1 · perf-solo 1

  $ gh api orgs/paiml/actions/runners --jq '[.runners[] | select([.labels[].name] | (index("macOS") or index("Windows") or index("macos")))] | length'
  0

  Twenty-five runners, every one Linux. ZERO macOS and ZERO Windows. So every
  Linux declaration can move and seven legs cannot.

### 4. the conversion

  Thirty-THREE declarations moved to [self-hosted, clean-room] across seventeen
  files. The script did 32, by script rather than by hand so that every site is
  the same edit; the 33rd is the one it missed, below. Re-derived from the trees
  rather than from the script's own report -- count the hosted-Linux labels that
  exist at 0bb6cb57 and no longer exist at HEAD:

      1  audit.yml          1  behavior.yml       2  bench.yml
      4  binary-release.yml 8  ci.yml             1  convergence.yml
      1  coverage.yml       1  docs.yml           2  lint.yml
      1  msrv.yml           1  mutation.yml       4  nightly.yml
      2  proofs.yml         1  quorum.yml         1  release-audit.yml
      1  release-goal.yml   1  stress.yml
    --- hosted-Linux runner labels that no longer exist: 33 across 17 files

  nightly.yml is 4 here and the script reported 3; lint.yml is 2 because its
  matrix held one ubuntu-latest and its second job another.

  THE SCRIPT MISSED ONE and the test caught it:

    nightly.yml:73  runner: ubuntu-24.04-arm   # native ARM64 build (GA, free for public repos)

  The converter matched a runner value to end-of-line; a trailing comment made
  the value `ubuntu-24.04-arm   # native ARM64 build (GA, free for public
  repos)`, which is not a hosted label by exact match. The falsification test
  parses the YAML, so the comment is not part of the value and the site was a
  finding. This is the whole argument for parsing over grepping, made by the
  change's own tooling against itself.

### 5. the two moves that needed more than a label

  nightly.yml built aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu with

    cargo build --release --features vendored-openssl --target ${{ matrix.target }}

  which works on a native ARM runner and cannot work on an x86_64 fleet host:
  no aarch64 linker, and vendored-openssl needs a cross C toolchain. release.yml
  has been building that same target on [self-hosted, clean-room] all along,
  with `cross build` for aarch64-unknown-linux-*. The leg now takes that path.

  binary-release.yml pinned its build leg to ubuntu-22.04 for glibc 2.35,
  because a binary linked against a newer glibc refuses to start on an older
  host. The pin is gone. Rather than assume the fleet's glibc, the workflow now
  RECORDS it. Verified locally, on a real forjar binary:

    $ bash <the new step, target substituted>
    builder: ldd (Ubuntu GLIBC 2.35-0ubuntu3.11) 2.35
    binary demands at most GLIBC_2.34

  and the same two lines into $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY. It never fails the build: a
  release that raises the floor is a decision for a reader, not for a step.

### 6. the comments that became false

  ci.yml:8      "security: cargo audit (ubuntu-latest, ...)"  -> (fleet, ...)
  ci.yml:176    justified the sudo test by GitHub's DOCUMENTED hosted-runner
                environment. GitHub documents that a hosted runner's `runner`
                user is non-root with passwordless sudo, and documents nothing
                about the fleet. The citation is gone; FORJAR_REQUIRE_SUDO_TESTS
                is what was ever load-bearing and is now the only half.
  binary-release.yml:56  "hosted runners only, no self-hosted dependency" --
                a deliberate choice this commit reverses, said so.
  binary-release.yml:100 the glibc pin, rewritten around the measurement above.

  release.yml:208 mentions macos-latest and is still true; it was left alone.

### 7. after

  $ grep -rn "runs-on:\|runner:\|os: \[" .github/workflows/ | grep -E "ubuntu|macos|windows"
  .github/workflows/nightly.yml:75:            runner: macos-latest
  .github/workflows/nightly.yml:77:            runner: macos-latest
  .github/workflows/nightly.yml:79:            runner: windows-latest
  .github/workflows/lint.yml (2 macos-latest legs, via include:)
  .github/workflows/release.yml:194:            runner: macos-latest
  .github/workflows/release.yml:196:            runner: macos-latest

  SEVEN legs, all of them a platform the fleet has no hardware for. Counted
  from the parsed YAML rather than from grep lines:

    lint.yml:macos-latest = 2
    nightly.yml:macos-latest = 2
    nightly.yml:windows-latest = 1
    release.yml:macos-latest = 2
    TOTAL LEGS: 7

  The grep above prints SIX lines, and the receipt, the log, the commit message,
  the test's doc comment and .github/actionlint.yaml all said "six" until this
  was counted properly. lint.yml contributes ONE line and TWO legs: its matrix
  used to be `os: [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]` on one line and this branch
  rewrote it as four explicit include: legs, two of which are macos-latest. The
  test's expected map had 7 all along -- it was the prose that was wrong, and
  the receipt contradicted itself, saying "Six legs" in one sentence and "seven
  legs" in the next.

  $ actionlint .github/workflows/*.yml   # findings across the directory
  base 0bb6cb57: 22      this branch: 15

  (.github/actionlint.yaml now declares the fleet's labels; without it
  actionlint reports every `clean-room` as an unknown label and the real
  findings are lost in the noise. The 15 that remain are pre-existing
  shellcheck infos inside run: blocks, none of them added here.)

