  92cb6a1a fix(ci): every CI job runs on the fleet, not on GitHub's runners (PMAT-547, closes #547)

  The tree is restored after each one (`git status --porcelain -- .github tests`
  is 0 lines at the end), and every mutation ran against the COMMITTED tree.

  RE-RUN AT 607e229b, after the review round grew the suite from four cases to
  ten. M1, M2 and M3 still kill exactly one case each. M4's anchor had moved --
  the review round rewrote `runner_labels` -- so it was re-aimed at
  `push_labels`, which is what every case now reads through:

    MUTATION M4: Value::String(s) => out.push(s.clone())  ->  Value::String(_) => {}
      the_object_form_of_runs_on_cannot_hide_a_hosted_runner ... FAILED
      a_fleet_job_is_not_mistaken_for_a_hosted_one ... ok
      a_hosted_label_in_another_case_is_still_a_hosted_label ... FAILED
      a_matrix_key_by_any_name_is_still_read ... FAILED
      an_expression_inside_a_runs_on_list_still_reaches_the_matrix ... FAILED
      no_linux_job_asks_github_for_a_runner ... ok
      the_platforms_the_fleet_cannot_serve_are_exactly_these ... FAILED
      a_fleet_job_names_a_pool_and_not_just_self_hosted ... ok
      the_jobs_that_delegate_their_runner_are_exactly_these ... ok
      the_parser_finds_the_runners_that_are_there ... FAILED

  M4 KILLS SIX, NOT ONE, and the earlier form of this log said "four single
  kills, no collateral". That was true of a four-case suite and is not true of
  this one. It is also the right answer: blinding the parser must break every
  case that reads through it, and the four survivors are precisely the four that
  assert an ABSENCE -- no Linux job is hosted, no fleet job is mistaken for one,
  no job names `self-hosted` bare, the delegated set is unchanged. A blinded
  parser reports nothing hosted, so all four pass while measuring nothing. That
  is the exact failure `the_parser_finds_the_runners_that_are_there` exists to
  catch, and M4 is the mutation that proves it catches it.

  So: three specific kills, and one mutation that demonstrates the vacuity
  guard is the only thing holding the other four honest.

### 10. `clean-room` spans two architectures

  Raised after the branch was already pushed, by measuring the fleet a second
  time -- this time by GROUP rather than by label:

  $ gh api orgs/paiml/actions/runner-groups --jq '.runner_groups[] | "\(.id) \(.name) visibility=\(.visibility)"'
  1 Default   visibility=all
  3 gpu-nodes visibility=selected
  5 gpu-x86   visibility=selected

  $ gh api orgs/paiml/actions/runner-groups/1/runners --jq '.runners[] | "\(.name) \([.labels[].name]|join(","))"'
  intel-clean-room      self-hosted,Linux,X64,clean-room,intel,build
  ... 16 of them, every one X64 ...

  $ gh api orgs/paiml/actions/runner-groups/3/runners --jq ...
  gx10-build   self-hosted,Linux,clean-room,ARM64,gx10,ephemeral,docker,build
  gx10-pool1   self-hosted,Linux,clean-room,ARM64,gx10,ephemeral,docker,build
  gx10-pool2   self-hosted,Linux,clean-room,ARM64,gx10,ephemeral,docker,build
  gx10-pool3   self-hosted,Linux,clean-room,ARM64,gx10,ephemeral,docker,build

  FOUR RUNNERS CARRY `clean-room` AND ARE ARM64. `[self-hosted, clean-room]`
  does not say X64, so the label alone does not choose an architecture.

  Nothing is wrong TODAY: groups 3 and 5 are `visibility=selected` and forjar is
  not in them, which is why every job this branch ran landed on an
  intel-clean-room box:

  $ for each run of this PR: jobs[].runner_name
  4 intel-clean-room-7 · 2 intel-clean-room-6 · intel-clean-room-8 ·
  intel-clean-room-15 · intel-clean-room

  But that is an ACCESS-CONTROL accident, not a property of the label. Adding
  forjar to `gpu-nodes` is one checkbox, and the day someone clicks it a job
  building `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` can be handed an ARM64 runner. The
  artifact it uploads would then be WRONG rather than missing, which is the
  worse of the two failures and the one a green check will not show.

  Every runner forjar can reach is X64, so naming it costs no capacity:

    50 declarations pinned to [self-hosted, clean-room, X64] across 18 files

  Seventeen of those fifty were already on the fleet before this branch --
  release.yml's ten and proofs.yml's -- and had the same unstated assumption.

  MUTATION M5: one fleet job drops its architecture
    a_fleet_job_names_the_architecture_it_needs ... FAILED
    (the other ten stay green; a single kill)

